Subject: [PATCH 1/6] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 2, patch 1;
 built on e6298271a with series 1 applied): NOTHING LEAVES A LEDGER WITHOUT A
 RECORDED ACT -- THE RULE, AND THE REGISTRY OF RETRACTED PHRASES (the seat's
 planted case PV11). With docs/RETRACTED_PHRASES.md deleted the sweep read no
 phrase, swept for nothing and passed. THE RULE, which the four patches after
 this one share: after NOTHING_LEAVES_FROM (main's e6298271a) what a ledger
 has held it still holds -- what it held at that commit, and anything added to
 it since on main's own line -- unless it is named in LEFT_BY_NAME with its
 date and what became of it. It is read from history, as append-only and
 seal-immutable are; it fails closed where the history cannot be read back to
 its anchor; and a named entry is counted in the gate's own sentence, which
 the runner now prints whole. A floor ('not empty') would catch the plant and
 miss half a page deleted. HERE: the registry must be there, and no phrase
 leaves it. The checker's reader is split so that the registry can be read as
 it was at an earlier commit; it reads the same 29 phrases in the same order
 as before. retraction-sweep now reads the past and moves in the table of
 series 1, whose own lock failed until it did. What stands before the anchor
 is not re-judged: on main no phrase has left the registry in a thousand
 commits. The lock: tests/test_nothing_leaves_a_ledger.py, five tests, each in
 a small repository of its own; on the commit before, the three on the
 registry fail because the sweep passes. 0 of 19.

